{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And all the congregation saw.</b> When they saw Moses and Eleazar descending and Aaron did not descend, they said: Where is Aaron? He said to them: He has died. They said: Is it possible that the one who stood against the angel and stopped the plague — the angel of death should rule over him? Immediately Moses sought mercy, and the ministering angels showed him to them, laid out on a bier. They saw and believed it (there)." "<b>All the house of Israel.</b> The men and the women — because Aaron pursued peace and cast love between disputants, and between a man and his wife (Avot de-Rabbi Natan 12)." "<b>For he is dead.</b> I say that the Targumist who rendered דְּהָא is mistaken — unless he translates וַיִּרְאוּ as וְאִתְחֲזִיאוּ, for our Sages did not say that כִּי serves in the sense of דְּהָא, but rather for the midrash that the clouds of glory withdrew, as Rabbi Abahu said (Rosh HaShanah 3): do not read וַיִּרְאוּ but וַיֵּרָאוּ — and on this reading the sense דְּהָא does apply, for it gives the reason for what precedes it: why וַיֵּרָאוּ? Because Aaron has died. But on the Targum 'וְחָזוּ כָל כְּנִשְׁתָּא,' the sense דְּהָא does not apply — only the sense of אֲשֶׁר, which derives from the usage of אִי, for we find אִם serving in the sense of אֲשֶׁר, as in ״וְאִם מַדּוּעַ לֹא תִקְצַר רוּחִי״ (Job 21), and many verses are explicit in this usage — ״אִם חֲרוּצִים יָמָיו״ (ibid. 14)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "כִּי גָוַע is translated as though punctuated כִּי גּוֹעַ to match the verse's accent; דְּהָא is the Aramaic demonstrative 'this/for this'; the Targum reference is to Onkelos on Numbers 20:29."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:54.789588065Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}